{
  "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DEShawResearch/random123/9545ff6413f258be2f04c1d319d99aaef7521150/tests/kat_vectors",
  "retrieved": "2026-11-06",
  "algorithm": "Philox4x32-10",
  "notes": "VALUES WERE RETRIEVED OVER HTTPS ON 2026-11-06 from the two official Random123 known-answer test vector files of D. E. Shaw Research (URLs and SHA-256 in the sources array); nothing here was written from memory or generated by this project. The files were also cross-checked against a second, independent copy of tests/kat_vectors (github.com/lemire/random123 examples/kat_vectors, byte-identical) and against a third-party Rust port that embeds the same three primary vectors with attribution to Random123 (github.com/pawlenartowicz/rand_philox, src/philox.rs tests). SPEC 14.9 INTERFACE: these are the permutation outputs of Philox4x32 with exactly 10 rounds, i.e. Philox4x32-10, NOT 4x32-7 and not any other variant. Both source files also contain philox4x32-7 rows (7 rounds) plus rows for other families (philox2x32, philox2x64, philox4x64, threefry2x32/4x32/2x64/4x64, aesni4x32, ars4x32); every non-4x32 row and every round-count != 10 row was excluded by the parser. The set field on each vector says which of the two files it came from; the three vectors from tests/kat_vectors come first, then the 52 vectors from tests/old_kat_vectors. HOW THE SOURCE SPELLS COUNTER/KEY/OUTPUTS: each file line is whitespace-separated hex, philox4x32 R ctr0 ctr1 ctr2 ctr3 key0 key1 r0 r1 r2 r3 -- 1 name + 1 round count + 4 counter words + 2 key words + 4 output words. Columns are already in array order: counter=[c0,c1,c2,c3], key=[K0,K1], expected=[r0,r1,r2,r3]. Values are given here as unsigned decimal integers; each counter/key/expected word is a u32 in 0..4294967295. There is NO endianness or word-order translation to apply: the library's own reader (tests/kat_main.h, macro RNGNxW_TPL) does strtou32 on the columns left-to-right into ctr.v[0..3] and key.v[0..nkey-1] and compares r123array4x32 word-by-word, and tests/README states that the kat vectors are also byte-order independent and the CBRNGs should produce the same numerical results on little-endian and big-endian hardware. Treat the words as numeric u32 values, never as a byte string. KEY WIDTH -- READ THIS BEFORE WRITING THE TEST (this is the bug class the fixture exists to catch): Random123 publishes the Philox4x32 key as TWO 32-BIT WORDS = a 64-bit key, NOT four words / 128 bits. This is the library's documented convention -- docs/cbrng.dox: Philox keys are half as wide as the counter -- and it is what the code says: include/Random123/philox.h defines philox4x32_key_t and philox4x32_ukey_t as r123array2x32 (2 x uint32_t), philox4x32keyinit() is the identity, the round is out = {hi1 ^ ctr.v[1] ^ key.v[0], lo1, hi0 ^ ctr.v[3] ^ key.v[1], lo0} with (hi0,lo0)=mulhilo32(0xD2511F53, ctr.v[0]) and (hi1,lo1)=mulhilo32(0xCD9E8D57, ctr.v[2]), and the key bump is the 32-bit wrapping add key.v[0] += 0x9E3779B9; key.v[1] += 0xBB67AE85 (PHILOX_W32_0/1). ROCm/rocRAND's philox4x32_10_engine, whose header says its constants and round are Source: Random123, agrees exactly (uint2 key). DERIVATION USED TO FILL THE key FIELD OF THIS FIXTURE (the source's spelling -> spec's [u64;2]): positional zero-extension -- key[0] = (u64) K0 and key[1] = (u64) K1, so the upper 32 bits of each u64 are 0 in every vector below. The spec's key:[u64;2] container therefore carries only 64 key bits of meaning for Philox4x32, and philox4x32_10() must feed only the LOW 32 bits of each u64 into the round as subkey words 0 and 1 respectively. The high halves can never influence any output: 32-bit wrapping addition carries upward only, so (K + W) mod 2^32 does not depend on bits 32..63 of K. Because zero-extension preserves a value, the source's own spelling and the derived [u64;2] spelling are the SAME two decimal numbers here -- the source does not publish four key words, it publishes two, so there is nothing further to convert; the verbatim hex line for each row is quoted in source_line. For whoever derives these keys from a seed (spec 14.12 stream keys): rocRAND splits its 64-bit seed the same way -- key.x = (u32)seed and key.y = (u32)(seed >> 32), default seed 0xdeadbeefdeadbeef -- so a 64-bit seed S becomes the pair [(u64)(S & 0xFFFFFFFF), (u64)(S >> 32)] in this fixture's spelling. Note that a 128-bit seed cannot be carried into these keys without changing the primitive. WHAT THESE VECTORS REJECT -- measured, not asserted. On 2026-11-06 each row was also run through deliberately-wrong variants of the reference algorithm; the count is how many of the 55 rows that variant still reproduces (0 = fully caught by the fixture, 55 = the correct algorithm): the wrong 128-bit key packing key=[K0 | (K1 << 32), 0], read as two low-32 subkeys, i.e. subkey 1 silently becomes zero -> 14 of 55; key words swapped [K1,K0] -> 2 of 55; key[0] ignored/zeroed -> 40 of 55; counter word order reversed -> 2 of 55; counter pairs swapped (c2,c3,c0,c1) -> 2 of 55; output words reversed -> 0 of 55; multipliers M4x32_0/M4x32_1 swapped -> 0 of 55; bump constants W32_0/W32_1 swapped -> 0 of 55; key bumped before every round including the first -> 0 of 55; key bumped only once instead of per round -> 0 of 55; 7 rounds -> 0 of 55; 11 rounds -> 0 of 55. Treating the two u64 words as full-width multiplicands is Philox4x64-10 (different constants: multipliers 0xD2E7470EE14C6C93 / 0xCA5A826395121157 and 64-bit bumps 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15 / 0xBB67AE8584CAA73B), a different primitive that fails every row here. Bumping the key one extra time AFTER the tenth round is NOT detectable and must not be tried: the final bump cannot affect the returned words. Note the two degenerate keys (all-zero and all-ones) are individually insensitive to subkey swapping, which is exactly why all 55 rows are vendored rather than only the 3 headline rows. WHAT THESE VECTORS CANNOT PIN: because the algorithm has no key bits above bit 31 of each u64, every published key has 0 in those high halves, so these vectors cannot detect a defect that lives exclusively in those dead bits. They cover the permutation only, not the counter's role as a stream address. If the engine means key to be a genuine 128-bit key, that is a different, unpublished primitive and it must be raised as a spec gap: the original authors publish no 128-bit-key Philox4x32 vectors. PROVENANCE OF THIS FILE'S OWN NUMBERS: every counter/key/expected below was parsed straight out of the two fetched files by a script (no hand transcription), and all 55 rows were re-derived from a throwaway Python re-implementation written only from the fetched philox.h and rocRAND header, with 55/55 matching before this fixture was written. The source_line field on each vector quotes the verbatim hex line from the fetched file so any reader can eyeball it. Per Random123's tests/README these are the vectors the BigCrush-validated reference implementation satisfies, so they are the algorithm freeze for this generator. The vectors remain the property of D. E. Shaw Research under Random123's 3-clause BSD-style license.",
